WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:11.480
<v Marta Oyelaran>So the honest answer is we don't know how many exceptions
we get, because the ones that matter never make it into the system.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.220
<v JP>When you say never make it in — is that people not recording them, or
no field to record them in?

3
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:31.050
<v Marta Oyelaran>Bit of both. There's a reason code list. It's fine. But if
a driver rings Tomasz and they sort it in two minutes, nobody's going back
to log it. And I'd rather they sorted it.

4
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.900
<v JP>What does an exception cost you when it goes wrong rather than right?

5
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:52.700
<v Marta Oyelaran>A failed delivery is about two hundred and forty pounds
all in. Redelivery, the driver's time, and the customer service call. We do
somewhere north of a hundred a month.

6
00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:02.300
<v JP>And the union — where do they sit on us watching the desk?

7
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:18.660
<v Marta Oyelaran>They'll want to be in the room. They're not against it.
What they are against is anything that looks like it's measuring Tomasz
against Yusuf. We had a productivity dashboard three years ago and it went
very badly.

8
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:27.400
<v Marta Oyelaran>Talk to Dawn. She's the rep. Go before you start, not
after.

9
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.900
<v JP>Which platform does your data sit on? Someone mentioned a warehouse.

10
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:49.100
<v Marta Oyelaran>That's a question for Yusuf, and I think the answer has
changed twice this year. Don't quote me.
